const rhodeCase="\u003carticle class=\"rhode-case\">\n\u003cdiv class=\"wrap\">\u003cheader class=\"page-hero reveal\">\u003ca class=\"link-arrow\" href=\"work.html\">Back to selected work \u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">↗\u003c/span>\u003c/a>\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">Independent Marketing Strategy &amp; Creative Concept\u003c/p>\u003ch1>Rhode — From Influence\u003cbr> to \u003cem>Involvement.\u003c/em>\u003c/h1>\u003cp class=\"lead\">A customer relationship worth building.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"rhode-disclaimer\">Independent speculative concept. Not commissioned by, affiliated with, or endorsed by Rhode. This concept is not affiliated with or endorsed by Hailey Bieber. All interfaces, event scenarios, customer comments, and proposed features are conceptual.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"actions\">\u003ca class=\"button\" href=\"https://sarahsaafwan.github.io/rhode-community/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Explore Interactive Prototype (opens in a new tab)\">Explore Interactive Prototype \u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">↗\u003c/span>\u003c/a>\u003ca class=\"link-arrow\" href=\"assets/rhode/Rhode_From_Influence_to_Involvement_Updated.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"View Strategy PDF (opens in a new tab)\">View Strategy PDF \u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">↗\u003c/span>\u003c/a>\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"rhode-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Brand studied\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Rhode\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Project status\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Self-initiated, unofficial concept\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Disciplines\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Marketing Strategy · Creative Direction · Customer Experience · Community Strategy · Digital Experience Design · Experiential Marketing · UX/UI Concept · Interactive Website\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/header>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\" aria-labelledby=\"interactive-prototype-title\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">Interactive website / Independent concept\u003c/p>\u003ch2 id=\"interactive-prototype-title\">An idea I wanted\u003cbr>people to \u003cem>actually try.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The Rhode Community project started with a question about how a beauty brand could make its customers feel more involved in what comes next. I developed the strategy, then built an interactive website concept to show how the experience could work, from customer feedback and product testing to community conversations and real-world events.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The prototype is independently developed and is not affiliated with Rhode. All activity is a browser-local demonstration.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"actions\">\u003ca class=\"button\" href=\"https://sarahsaafwan.github.io/rhode-community/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Explore Interactive Prototype (opens Rhode Community in a new tab)\">Explore Interactive Prototype \u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">↗\u003c/span>\u003c/a>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003cfigure class=\"rhode-visual\">\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/landing.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Enlarge the proposed Rhode community landing page\">\u003cimg src=\"assets/rhode/landing.jpg\" alt=\"Proposed Rhode community landing page with a customer invitation, founder Q&amp;A, and participation areas\" width=\"1280\" height=\"714\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\">\u003c/a>\u003cfigcaption>\u003cspan>A dedicated space for the people who know Rhode best.\u003c/span>\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/landing.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View full size ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">01 / Introduction\u003c/p>\u003ch2>The visibility.\u003cbr>\u003cem>The relationship.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Rhode has built an instantly recognizable world around beauty, personal influence, visual consistency, and cultural relevance. Its founder-led identity and focused product philosophy give the brand a distinctive place in contemporary beauty.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The opportunity I explore here is what happens after that visibility becomes a purchase. How could Rhode develop deeper relationships with its everyday customers, giving them meaningful access to conversations, product development, and experiences?\u003c/p>\u003cp>This is an independent strategic proposal. It studies the brand’s public-facing world and develops a possible next layer of customer participation; it does not describe work delivered for Rhode.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">02 / The observation\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Access is not the same\u003cbr>as \u003cem>participation.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Rhode’s influencer-driven visibility is one of its strengths. The brand’s most recognizable advocates, however, are not necessarily the same people as its broader customer base.\u003c/p>\u003cp>My hypothesis is that a more direct relationship with verified customers could complement this influence. The proposal asks whether people who buy and use Rhode could become active contributors, with access that does not depend on the size of their own audience.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This is not a claim that Rhode overlooks customers or lacks customer research. It is a question about how participation might become more visible, accessible, and continuous within the brand experience.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">03 / The idea\u003c/p>\u003ch2>A place in Rhode\u003cbr>customers can \u003cem>shape.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>A verified-customer community integrated directly into Rhode’s existing website. Eligible customers could express opinions, submit ideas, participate in product feedback, attend live conversations, apply for testing opportunities, and follow how their contributions are considered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The community would need standards as much as aesthetics: verified, not viral; honest, not polished; useful, not performative. Constructive criticism should have a place alongside enthusiasm. Participation should lead to review, testing, decisions, or a clear explanation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">04 / The digital ecosystem\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Something to see,\u003cbr>use, and \u003cem>return to.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The early exploration connects desktop and mobile participation: conversations, co-creation, testing applications, benefits, events, and a visible feedback journey. It is an overview of the wider vision, rather than a finished interface specification.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cfigure class=\"rhode-visual\">\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/overview.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Enlarge the early Rhode customer ecosystem exploration\">\u003cimg src=\"assets/rhode/overview.jpg\" alt=\"Early exploratory overview of Rhode desktop and mobile community, verification, live Q&amp;A, discussions, testing, rewards, events, and feedback journeys\" width=\"1280\" height=\"714\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\u003c/a>\u003cfigcaption>\u003cspan>Early exploration of a customer-centered Rhode digital ecosystem.\u003c/span>\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/overview.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View full size ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Four functions organize the experience. \u003cstrong>Ask:\u003c/strong> live questions and archived conversations. \u003cstrong>Shape:\u003c/strong> product requests, feedback, voting, and visible statuses. \u003cstrong>Test:\u003c/strong> structured, inclusive opportunities to try suitable prototypes. \u003cstrong>Belong:\u003c/strong> participation benefits and experiences beyond a launch calendar.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">05 / Customer access and verification\u003c/p>\u003ch2>A relationship to the brand.\u003cbr>\u003cem>A voice in the community.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cfigure class=\"rhode-visual\">\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/verification.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Enlarge the proposed customer verification screen\">\u003cimg src=\"assets/rhode/verification.jpg\" alt=\"Conceptual Rhode customer verification screen offering direct-purchase account access and an authorized-retailer purchase route\" width=\"1280\" height=\"714\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\u003c/a>\u003cfigcaption>\u003cspan>Membership begins with a genuine relationship to the brand.\u003c/span>\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/verification.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View full size ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The proposed entry process checks eligibility through a direct-purchase account or a separate authorized-retailer route. The strategic principle is customership: access begins with buying and using the products, rather than follower count.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every verified member could contribute ideas, join discussions, vote, attend digital sessions, and receive updates. Further opportunities, such as selected trials or invitations, would need transparent criteria that recognize meaningful participation without making spending the measure of whose feedback matters.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The verification flow is conceptual. Retailer integrations, evidence requirements, privacy, accessibility, and support would need validation before development. Retailer names shown in the mockup illustrate an access route and do not imply confirmed technical partnerships.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">06 / Founder access and engagement\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Lead with a\u003cbr>\u003cem>live conversation.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cfigure class=\"rhode-visual\">\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/live-qa.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Enlarge the proposed Hailey live Q&amp;A interface\">\u003cimg src=\"assets/rhode/live-qa.jpg\" alt=\"Conceptual live community Q&amp;A with Hailey Bieber, member questions, replay access, and follow-up topics\" width=\"1280\" height=\"714\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\u003c/a>\u003cfigcaption>\u003cspan>A more direct conversation between Hailey and Rhode’s customers.\u003c/span>\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/live-qa.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View full size ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The proposed inaugural event is a live Q&amp;A with Hailey Bieber and the Rhode team for verified customers. Questions come from members, with follow-ups and active moderation. A conversation should allow uncertainty, disagreement, and answers that are not ready yet.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This format demonstrates the relationship being proposed more clearly than a highly scripted promotional film. A direct announcement could introduce the invitation without staged reactions or an emotional montage: one camera, one conversation, one invitation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The conversation should remain useful afterward. Recordings and unanswered questions could stay accessible, with a visible list of what the team is following up on. This is a proposed event, not a confirmed appearance; the comments and responses in the screen are illustrative.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">07 / Transparency and participation\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Conversations that\u003cbr>\u003cem>lead somewhere.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cfigure class=\"rhode-visual\">\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/discussions.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Enlarge the proposed Rhode discussions and feedback screen\">\u003cimg src=\"assets/rhode/discussions.jpg\" alt=\"Proposed Rhode discussion board showing customer criticism, product ideas, conceptual team replies, and progress from feedback to decisions\" width=\"1280\" height=\"714\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\u003c/a>\u003cfigcaption>\u003cspan>Customer feedback should lead to conversation—and visible follow-through.\u003c/span>\u003ca href=\"assets/rhode/discussions.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View full size ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Customers could discuss product sizes, shades, packaging, and experiences openly. Criticism, suggestions, and team responses should remain visible within a respectful, actively moderated community.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Product-development suggestions and voting could help identify questions worth exploring. A progress view would connect submissions to review, appropriate testing, and a decision. Not every idea becomes a product; explaining why something does not progress is part of the trust mechanism.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Testing opportunities would require qualified product teams, appropriate safety review, inclusive recruitment, and structured feedback. Votes would inform consideration, not replace formulation expertise or promise a launch.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">08 / Beyond the screen\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Participation has a\u003cbr>\u003cem>physical dimension.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Community and loyalty benefits could connect ongoing participation to selected trials, workshops, and customer-first gatherings. Events could bring together newer and long-term customers, with free access opportunities and paid workshops where appropriate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The experiences should offer something to do or understand: explore packaging, shades, and storytelling; learn from the team; or join supervised demonstrations where feasible. Their value would extend beyond a place to take photographs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>International events and product experiences would depend on customer demand, distribution, local feasibility, and suitable partners. The concept proposes a direction, not confirmed destinations, invitations, or operational capabilities.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">09 / Creative direction\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Unmistakably Rhode.\u003cbr>\u003cem>A new way to participate.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The aim is to extend Rhode’s visual identity into a new digital experience. Warm neutrals, clean typography, minimal layouts, thoughtful photography, and accessible interactions give the proposed community a recognizable place within the brand.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The landing page makes the invitation clear; verification gives access a rationale; the live event makes interaction tangible; and discussions make follow-through visible. Each screen serves a strategic role beyond its visual treatment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">10 / Business rationale and rollout\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Start with credibility.\u003cbr>\u003cem>Earn the right to expand.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>The initiative explores stronger customer relationships, returning participation, retention, customer-led product insights, and a more balanced approach to brand access. These are strategic objectives, not proven outcomes.\u003c/p>\u003col class=\"rhode-steps\">\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Validate.\u003c/strong> Research customer needs and feasibility; define verification, privacy, moderation, and participation rules.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Open.\u003c/strong> Pilot the member hub, a live founder conversation, and visible feedback updates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prove.\u003c/strong> Assess useful participation, structured trials, decision statuses, and benefits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Expand.\u003c/strong> Explore physical experiences and selected international pilots only where evidence and feasibility support them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">11 / Proposed measurement\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Being worth\u003cbr>\u003cem>listening to.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>A pilot would need agreed baselines, practical definitions, and appropriate consent. Potential indicators include verified membership, meaningful participation, returning members, customer satisfaction, and whether members feel heard.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Operational measures could track feedback response times, suggestions reaching a visible decision, product feedback acted upon, and testing participation. Community-event attendance and repeat purchases could be considered alongside retention, with care around attribution.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No campaign has been implemented through this concept. There are no reported performance results, invented research findings, or promised conversion improvements.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003csection class=\"section rhode-editorial\">\u003cp class=\"eyebrow\">12 / My conclusion\u003c/p>\u003ch2>From being seen\u003cbr>to being \u003cem>involved.\u003c/em>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"rhode-prose\">\u003cp>Rhode does not need to replace influence. This proposal asks how involvement could become another part of what the brand stands for: a relationship that gives customers a way to contribute and a reason to return.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Further customer, competitor, legal, privacy, and feasibility research would be needed before implementation. Public brand references used in the original study include \u003ca href=\"https://www.rhodeskin.com/pages/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rhode’s brand story\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"https://www.rhodeskin.com/products/pocket-blush-piggy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pocket Blush\u003c/a>, and the \u003ca href=\"https://www.rhodeskin.com/collections/shop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">product collection\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"rhode-disclaimer\">Self-initiated and unofficial. This project was not commissioned, approved, developed, or implemented by Rhode. It does not represent an employment, consultancy, collaboration, or campaign relationship.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"actions\">\u003ca class=\"button\" href=\"https://sarahsaafwan.github.io/rhode-community/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Explore Interactive Prototype (opens in a new tab)\">Explore Interactive Prototype \u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">↗\u003c/span>\u003c/a>\u003ca class=\"button\" href=\"assets/rhode/Rhode_From_Influence_to_Involvement_Updated.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">View Strategy PDF ↗\u003c/a>\u003ca class=\"link-arrow\" href=\"work.html\">Back to selected work ↗\u003c/a>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\n\u003c/div>\u003c/article>\n";
const reflections=[{"category": "podcast", "name": "R. Mneimneh", "detail": "Student · Lebanon", "label": "The Sae Talk", "rating": 5, "quote": "I really enjoy The Sae Talk Podcast because it simply helps me feel seen. The thing that made me ecstatic about it is how every experience mentioned in each episode sounds so familiar, and it really helps me mentally by making me feel like, \"Don't worry, we're all on the same page.\" The way each topic feels so relatable really caught me off guard and made me think, \"I should listen to this. I can relate!\"\n\nI would recommend everyone to check out the podcast because it's so helpful in changing your mentality and perspective, as well as helping you achieve a better lifestyle.\n\nThere's also a quote from the gratitude episode that has stayed with me and become my favorite:\n\n\"Gratitude isn't about appreciating everything around us. It's simply a verb, it's about acting grateful.\"", "words": ["Amazing", "Thoughtful", "Lovely"], "notes": ["Listener reflection", "Reviewed in July 2026"]}, {"category": "substack", "name": "Y. A.", "detail": "Content Creator · Cairo", "label": "Sarah Safwan — Substack", "rating": 5, "quote": "You explain small feelings in a way that makes them feel less random.", "words": [], "notes": ["Reader note"]}, {"category": "website", "name": "Anonymous", "detail": "Website visitor", "label": "Website", "rating": 5, "quote": "The website feels calm, thoughtful, and very personal. It gives a clear sense of who Sarah is before you even reach the contact section.", "words": [], "notes": ["Portfolio review"]}, {"category": "podcast", "name": "Mo S.", "detail": "", "label": "The Sae Talk", "rating": 5, "quote": "I came across Sarah’s work through her podcast and eventually found myself exploring her website and writing as well. What stood out to me most was how intentional everything feels. There’s a sense of thoughtfulness behind every piece of content that makes it feel personal rather than performative.\n\nI especially enjoyed the way complex ideas are broken down into reflections that are easy to relate to. Instead of simply sharing advice, Sarah invites you to think differently about your own experiences, which is something I found refreshing.\n\nOne idea that stayed with me was that growth doesn’t always come from having all the answers, it often begins with asking better questions. That perspective has genuinely stuck with me.\n\nI would absolutely recommend her work to anyone who enjoys thoughtful conversations, meaningful writing, and content that prioritizes depth over noise. It’s the kind of work you don’t just consume, you sit with it afterward.", "words": [], "notes": ["Listener reflection", "Reviewed in July 2026"]}];
const escapeText=text=>String(text).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
const reflectionCard=(item,index)=>`<article class="reflection-card" data-reflection-category="${item.category}"><div class="reflection-person-head"><div><h3 data-no-translate>${escapeText(item.name)}</h3>${item.detail?`<p class="review-note">${escapeText(item.detail)}</p>`:''}<span class="reflection-stars" aria-label="5 out of 5 stars">★★★★★</span></div><span class="pill" data-no-translate>${escapeText(item.label)}</span></div>${item.words.length?`<div class="reflection-words" data-no-translate>${item.words.map(word=>`<span>${escapeText(word)}</span>`).join('')}</div>`:''}<blockquote class="reflection-quote ${item.quote.length>350?'reflection-collapsed':''}" id="reflection-quote-${index}" data-no-translate>${escapeText(item.quote)}</blockquote>${item.quote.length>350?`<button type="button" class="text-button" data-reflection-expand="reflection-quote-${index}" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="reflection-quote-${index}">Read more</button>`:''}<div class="reflection-notes">${item.notes.map(note=>`<span class="eyebrow">${escapeText(note)}</span>`).join('')}</div></article>`;
const reflectionsGallery=(full=false)=>`<div class="reflections-gallery">${full?`<div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter feedback">${[['all','All reflections'],['podcast','The Sae Talk'],['substack','Sarah Safwan — Substack'],['website','Website']].map(([category,label],i)=>`<button type="button" data-feedback-filter="${category}" aria-pressed="${i===0}">${label}</button>`).join('')}</div>`:''}<div class="reflections-grid">${(full?reflections:[reflections[0],reflections[1]]).map(reflectionCard).join('')}</div><div class="approved-feedback" data-approved-feedback></div></div>`;

